This compression is not a paradox; it is a direct consequence of the heuristic being good (low error) and the true path being short.  

**C. f‑value spread → frontier size (memory)**  
- A* expands nodes in order of increasing f. When f‑values are well spread, the set of nodes sharing the current best f is small, so the open set stays small (peak ~5–12 for DFS mazes).  
- When f‑values are compressed, **many nodes have the same f** (or differ by <1). A* must store all of them because any could lie on an optimal path. The open set balloons (peak ~44–277 for BFS mazes, depending on size).  

This is the **f‑value condensation effect**, first described by Pearl (1984) for uniform‑cost search with a consistent heuristic. It is the root cause of A*’s memory weakness on BFS‑type mazes.  

**D. Absolute expansions are roughly equal**  
