## 2. Problem Analysis

### 2.1 The User’s Goal

The user asked to *“design a search algorithm better than A*”* and to *“test and benchmark it against other search types in both dfs and bfs type mazes”* with *“all metrics should be considered.”* The agreed evaluation framework (Understanding dimension) defined “better than A*” via:

1. **Hard constraints**: optimality ≤1.10× optimal, memory ≤2× A* peak, runtime ≤2× A* median, success ≥95%.
2. **Pareto dominance** (Tier 2): improve at least two of the four core metrics (runtime, memory, optimality, success) without worsening any beyond the hard constraints.
3. **Aggregate score** (Tier 3): mean of normalized ratios for runtime, memory, optimality, success.

### 2.2 Maze Taxonomy

Both maze types are **perfect spanning trees** (zero cycles; exactly one path between any two cells):

| Property | DFS‑Type (Recursive Backtracker) | BFS‑Type (Randomized Prim’s) |
|----------|----------------------------------|------------------------------|
| Tree shape | Deep, winding “river” | Shallow, bushy |
| Solution path length / N | 0.55 – 0.75 | 0.25 – 0.45 |
| Mean branching factor | 2.0 – 2.5 | 2.8 – 3.5 |
| Dead‑end count / N | 0.10 – 0.18 | 0.20 – 0.32 |
| Manhattan heuristic quality | Poor (underestimates by 30–60%) | Good (underestimates by 10–20%) |

### 2.3 The False Diagnosis

During the **Inquiry dimension**, we observed that A*’s open set on BFS mazes was far larger than on DFS mazes (e.g., 42 nodes vs 4 nodes for 51×51). We also observed that the **f‑values** of expanded nodes on BFS mazes had a very narrow spread (coefficient of variation ≈ 0.002) while those on DFS mazes had a wide spread (CV ≈ 0.34). This led to the causal narrative:

> *Heuristic accuracy → f‑value condensation → large open set → memory bottleneck.*

**This was correlation mistaken for causation.** The sandbox evidence (Grounding dimension) revealed that the **true‑cost** distribution (f*) is *wider* on BFS mazes than on DFS mazes (CV 0.17 vs 0.07 for 51×51). The narrow f‑spread observed in A* is an artifact of *early search sampling*: A* reaches the goal quickly on shallow BFS trees and never explores the full variance of the space. The real cause of the large open set is **topological uniformity** – a shallow, bushy tree has many nodes with true cost close to optimal, so A* must store them all. The heuristic merely fails to *disrupt* this uniformity.

This misdiagnosis led the committee down a three‑dimension dead end (Creative, Procedural, CodeObservation) pursuing adaptive switching algorithms that attacked a symptom rather than the root cause.

