## 3. Failed Adaptive Approaches — A Cautionary Tale

### 3.1 The Three Proposals

| Algorithm | Core Idea | Why It Failed in Sandbox |
|-----------|-----------|--------------------------|
| **FAS** (F‑Adaptive Search) | Compute CV of f‑values over a sliding window; switch to run‑length‑encoded open set or frontierless walk when CV < threshold. | CV threshold (0.15) was never triggered on BFS mazes because early‑search f‑spread is not the same as full‑landscape f‑spread. On DFS mazes the early CV was also low, so the algorithm misclassified and added overhead. |
| **Φ* v2** (F‑Bucketed Search) | Partition open set into f‑buckets; store only the lowest bucket explicitly; iteratively increase bound if goal not found. | Lossy bucketing forced repeated re‑searches from root; the BFS maze generator (wall‑list Prim) took 8.8 s for a single 201×201 maze, causing sandbox timeouts. On DFS mazes the search overhead exceeded 2× A* runtime. |
| **MAFS** (Meta‑Adaptive Frontierless Search) | Abandon the open set entirely; use a biased random walk with restarts guided by local f‑values. | Stochastic behaviour provided no hard runtime guarantee; the probability of finding the goal within the bound on large BFS mazes fell below 95%. |

### 3.2 Why All Adaptive Designs Are Fundamentally Brittle

The core flaw is **timing‑inverted detection**. The peak open set (the memory bottleneck) occurs *early* in the search – often within the first few hundred expansions. Reliable detection of condensation requires a larger sample (e.g., 500–1000 expansions), but by then the memory has already peaked. Any detection‑based scheme is either too early (noisy, misclassifies) or too late (the problem has already occurred).

Additionally, the metric **γ = open_set_size / expansions** (used by DMG‑GAS and GAS) is **scale‑dependent**. On BFS 25×25, γ after 200 expansions is ~0.05–0.07; on BFS 101×101 it is also ~0.05–0.07. But on DFS 25×25 it is ~0.02–0.03, and on DFS 101×101 it is ~0.01. A single threshold cannot cleanly separate the two across all grid sizes. The committee’s attempts to calibrate (0.10, 0.08, 0.06) inevitably failed on one configuration or another.

